## 3. Layer 1: Hard Constraints (Elimination Logic)

Each candidate \( i \) is evaluated against a boolean eligibility function before any scoring occurs:

\[
\text{Eligible}(i, v) = A(i,v) \land \lnot E(i, fam_v) \land R(i, v) \land G(i, v)
\]

Where, for visit \( v \) and candidate \( i \):

- \( A(i,v) \) — availability indicator (1 if candidate's calendar has an open slot overlapping visit window, else 0)
- \( E(i, fam_v) \) — exclusion indicator (1 if candidate is on the family's exclusion list, e.g., dual-relationship case)
- \( R(i, v) \) — role/certification match indicator (1 if candidate holds required certification for visit type)
- \( G(i, v) \) — logistics feasibility indicator (1 if candidate can fulfill transport requirements, e.g., van certification)

Only candidates where \( \text{Eligible}(i, v) = \text{True} \) proceed to Layer 2.

**Example (Twins Case, 5034/5035):**

| Candidate | Available? | Excluded? | Certified? | Van OK? | Eligible? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Emma | Yes | **Yes** (dual relationship) | Yes | Yes | **No** |
| Kali | Yes | No | Yes | Yes | **Yes** |
| Axie | No (no overlap) | No | Yes | Yes | **No** |

Only Kali passes Layer 1 — consistent with the actual outcome where Sanjana scheduled Kali for both twins after mom's comfort was confirmed.

