## 8. Implementation Notes

- Weights \( w_1 \)–\( w_5 \) should be stored as a versioned configuration table so the PI/lab manager can adjust them without code changes, and so past assignments can be audited against the weights active at assignment time.
- Layer 1 filters must run first and are non-negotiable — no weight combination should be able to override an exclusion flag, matching the lab's actual practice of treating dual-relationship cases as hard stops.
- The composite score formula is linear and interpretable by design (rather than a black-box model), so coordinators can explain to the PI exactly why a given candidate was recommended.
- Recommend a "confidence margin" alert: if the top two candidates' scores differ by less than \( \epsilon \), flag the assignment for a coordinator's quick manual glance even if not a strict tie, since close scores often indicate genuinely ambiguous cases (as in the worked example above).
